background:
New Zealand uses the [mixed-member proportional voting system](https://elections.nz/democracy-in-nz/what-is-new-zealands-system-of-government/what-is-mmp) to elect its House of Representatives. Voters can choose both a political party and a local candidate. Political parties that receive at least 5% of the party vote or at least one electorate seat receive seats in the House in proportion to the percentage of the party vote they receive. At this election, 71 of the 120 seats will be filled by the MPs elected from the electorate seats, determined by the first-past-the-post method. The remaining 49 seats will be filled by candidates from each party's closed party list. If a party wins more electorates than seats, extra seats may be filled to cover the "overhang".

Since the introduction of this system in 1996, only once has a party won an outright majority of seats ([the Labour Party in 2020](https://archive.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/)). Usually, parties negotiate to form a coalition or minority government. Pursuant to the [Government Formation](https://www.dpmc.govt.nz/our-business-units/cabinet-office/supporting-work-cabinet/cabinet-manual/6-elections-transitions-and-government-formation/government-formation) section of New Zealand’s Cabinet Manual, a government is formed when it is appointed by the Governor-General.&#x20;

The [current government](https://www3.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/house-seating-plan/) is led by the National Party with support from ACT and NZ First. The 2026 general election is expected to be [a tight race](https://election2026.nz/), with the National Party stating [they would not govern with the Opportunity Party](https://www.rnz.co.nz/news/politics/809555/national-rules-out-working-with-opportunity-party) and [the polls varying](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opinion_polling_for_the_2026_New_Zealand_general_election).&#x20;
